  "notes": [
   "Matrix wiring uses the tutorial table (matrix pin -> row/column -> Arduino pin) mapped onto the catalogue led-matrix-8x8 ports: R1..R8 = matrix pins 9,14,8,12,1,7,2,5 and C1..C8 = pins 13,3,4,10,6,11,15,16.",
   "Eight matrix lines go through 1k resistors (Arduino pins 2-9, yellow wires): 2->R1, 3->C4, 4->C6, 5->R4, 6->C1, 7->R2, 8->C7, 9->C8. The resistor leg plugs into the Arduino header and a jumper wire runs from the other leg to the matrix, as in the image.",
   "The other eight lines are direct green wires: 13->R5, 12->R7, 11->C2, 10->C3, A2->R8, A3->C5, A4->R6, A5->R3.",
   "Breadboard positions for the potentiometers and the rail holes are read from the image and are approximate. The breadboard's right-hand rails are modelled as the 'tp' (+) and 'tn' (-) rails.",
   "Pot 1 (upper, rows 8-10) wiper goes to A1. Pot 2 (lower, rows 13-15) wiper goes to A0. Each pot's outer legs go to the + and - rails with short wires.",
   "The Arduino 5V and GND header pins feed the breadboard rails. The first GND pin is assumed to be GND.1.",
   "The 2D matrix is not on the breadboard, so wires connect directly to its pins. Wire-to-resistor-lead joins are modelled as clip/lead joins."
  ]
 },
